Z||000025||maryjane||10-25-2001||10:33 PM||||Now I'm no physics expert, so this is probably full of holes. Maybe dark has mass, takes up space just as air, water & Fieros. If you dip out a cup of water from a pail, the molecules of water 'nearest the void moves in first, then the molecules nearest the first ones move inward toward the void, until you reach the outermost molecules, which move almost imperceptivly. That's why if you ignite a small candle in a very large darkened room, it only sucks in the dark in the immediate area of the candle first. But if you had a very sensitive device to measure, you would see that a minute amount of dark was sucked in from even the fartherest reaches of the room. You can be as technical and scientific as you desire in your rebuttal, I won't understand it anyway. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ||||reg||1
